09967bc1-0423-5b47-bfb1-a09932f81475.content:
Section: C: Classes and class hierarchies

Rule: C.90: Rely on constructors and assignment operators, not memset and memcpy

Reason: The standard C++ mechanism to construct an instance of a type is to call its constructor. As specified in guideline 
C.41
: a constructor should create a fully initialized object. No additional initialization, such as by 
memcpy
, should be required.
A type will provide a copy constructor and/or copy assignment operator to appropriately make a copy of the class, preserving the type’s invariants. Using memcpy to copy a non-trivially copyable type has undefined behavior. Frequently this results in slicing, or data corruption.

Example:
struct base {
    virtual void update() = 0;
    std::shared_ptr<int> sp;
};

struct derived : public base {
    void update() override {}
};

Example:
void init(derived& a)
{
    memset(&a, 0, sizeof(derived));
}

Example:
void copy(derived& a, derived& b)
{
    memcpy(&a, &b, sizeof(derived));
}

Enforcement: Flag passing a non-trivially-copyable type to 
memset
 or 
memcpy
.

