a34c0347-a13c-586a-be43-4f3278391c64.content:
Section: I: Interfaces

Rule: I.7: State postconditions

Reason: To detect misunderstandings about the result and possibly catch erroneous implementations.

Example:
int area(int height, int width) { return height * width; }  // bad

int area(int height, int width)
{
    auto res = height * width;
    Ensures(res > 0);
    return res;
}

Example:
void f()    // problematic
{
    char buffer[MAX];
    // ...
    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));
}

void f()    // better
{
    char buffer[MAX];
    // ...
    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));
    Ensures(buffer[0] == 0);
}

Example:
mutex m;

void manipulate(Record& r)    // don't
{
    m.lock();
    // ... no m.unlock() ...
}

void manipulate(Record& r)    // postcondition: m is unlocked upon exit
{
    m.lock();
    // ... no m.unlock() ...
}

void manipulate(Record& r)    // best
{
    lock_guard<mutex> _ {m};
    // ...
}

Enforcement: (Not enforceable) This is a philosophical guideline that is infeasible to check
directly in the general case. Domain specific checkers (like lock-holding
checkers) exist for many toolchains.

09967bc1-0423-5b47-bfb1-a09932f81475.content:
Section: C: Classes and class hierarchies

Rule: C.90: Rely on constructors and assignment operators, not memset and memcpy

Reason: The standard C++ mechanism to construct an instance of a type is to call its constructor. As specified in guideline 
C.41
: a constructor should create a fully initialized object. No additional initialization, such as by 
memcpy
, should be required.
A type will provide a copy constructor and/or copy assignment operator to appropriately make a copy of the class, preserving the type’s invariants. Using memcpy to copy a non-trivially copyable type has undefined behavior. Frequently this results in slicing, or data corruption.

Example:
struct base {
    virtual void update() = 0;
    std::shared_ptr<int> sp;
};

struct derived : public base {
    void update() override {}
};

Example:
void init(derived& a)
{
    memset(&a, 0, sizeof(derived));
}

Example:
void copy(derived& a, derived& b)
{
    memcpy(&a, &b, sizeof(derived));
}

Enforcement: Flag passing a non-trivially-copyable type to 
memset
 or 
memcpy
.

